and with no retained children, the only way a required symbol could be lost is if the root (which is required) is removed? No. But still, to be safe, count occurrences of each required symbol in retained set (including the current node). If the node's symbol is not required, then removing it does not affect count of required symbols. So condition is automatically satisfied. However, consider scenario where a node has symbol that is not required, but its subtree contains a required symbol? But we only remove node if it has no retained children. So if it had a child with required symbol, that child would be retained (since required symbols are never removed), so the node would have a retained child, thus cannot be removed. So indeed the condition holds. We can skip check for simplicity.\n        - Remove node: retained.remove(node_id); removed_order.append(node_id).\n7. After processing all nodes, get sorted retained list = sorted(retained).\n8. Return dict: {\"retained\": sorted_retained, \"removed\": removed_order}.\n\nBut need to verify with given fixtures. The fixtures have nodes with \"removable\" flag. For root, removable false. For n1 (symbol s1, required) removable false. For n2 removable true, symbol s2 not required, has children? n4 is child of n2. n4 is removable true but its symbol is s1 (required). So n2 has a child n4 that is retained initially. So n2 cannot be removed because it has retained child. n3 has child n5, both not required? n3 symbol s0, not required; n5 symbol s2 not required but n5 removable false. So n3 has retained child n5, cannot remove. n4 (child of n2) symbol s1 required, cannot remove because required symbol. n5 (child of n3) removable false, skip. n6 (child of n1) symbol s0 not required, removable true, and has no children. n1 is retained. So n6 can be removed? But check condition: n6 symbol s0 not required, removable true, no retained children (leaf), not root. Also required symbols: root and s1. Removing n6 does not affect root or s1. So n6 should be removed. That matches typical reduction? Possibly. But also n2 might become removable after n4 is removed? But n4 cannot be removed due to required symbol. So n2 remains with retained child n4, so cannot remove. So only n6 is removed in this example? The first fixture: after processing, retained IDs: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5 (n6 removed). Sorted retained: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]? Not exactly, root is first lexicographically? \"n\" > \"r\"? Actually 'n' ASCII 110, 'r' 114, so \"n1\" < \"root\" because 'n' < 'r'. So sorted order: \"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\". Removed order: [\"n6\"].\n\nBut is that correct? The algorithm also considers \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but the problem says we don't have actual grammar, so we assume all deletions are grammar-valid? The fixture has no grammar. So we just follow the rules as given.\n\nWe need to ensure we never remove a node that is required by symbol. The rule says \"Never remove ... a required symbol\". So n4 (s1) cannot be removed even if removable true. So that's correct.\n\nNow, what about nodes that are not removable? They are skipped.\n\nOne more nuance: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" We sort by depth descending. That means leaves (depth 2) first. Then depth 1, then depth 0. That ensures we process children before parents.\n\nThe tie-break: \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" Sorting by node ID ascending gives stable tie break.\n\nNow, the output: \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" I'll produce a dict with keys \"retained\" and \"removed\". The problem statement might expect specific key names like \"retained_ids\" and \"removal_order\"? But the description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" I'll use \"retained\" (list) and \"removed\" (list). Also need to ensure the function returns a JSON-serializable object. So a dict.\n\nEdge cas